cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Laurel: a scanned corpus of English verse, with per-line stress, identified
  meter, rhyme, and canonical references
message: "If you use this corpus, please cite it."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Dome
    given-names: Garrett
repository-code: "https://github.com/laurel-corpus/laurel-corpus"
# The concept DOI always resolves to the newest version. Cite the version DOI below when a result
# depends on exactly these texts.
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22738547"
version: v1.03
date-released: 2026-09-18
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.22738547"
    description: "All versions of the Laurel corpus"
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.22738548"
    description: "Version v1.01"
url: "https://laurelpoetry.com"
abstract: >-
  Laurel is a corpus of public domain English poetry, encoded in TEI P5, in which every
  line has been scanned. Every line carries two rows of marks: the stress the meter asks
  for, and the stress the words themselves require, with the syllables that neither
  settles left open rather than guessed at. Stanzas carry a rhyme scheme lettered from
  rhyme keys, and every poem has a canonical reference on the CTS model that names its
  edition, since a citation to a poem is not reproducible unless it says which text it
  means.


  The texts are in the public domain. The encoding, the scansion and the annotation are CC
  BY-SA 4.0. METHOD.md in the repository documents the method and the places where it is
  weak, including how accurate the scansion has been measured to be, the limits of the
  selection and, in this release, two verse dramas whose division into sections is wrong.


keywords:
